<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Will I eventually get used to the occlusion effect?</h3>
<p>Some people adapt a little over a week or two, but strong occlusion rarely disappears on its own. If your voice still sounds boomy or echoey after a short adjustment period, ask your audiologist to adjust venting, fit depth, or programming. These changes typically provide immediate relief.</p>

<h3>Is an open dome always better for my own voice?</h3>
<p>Open domes often help, but they aren’t ideal for every hearing loss—especially when you need more low-frequency amplification or have feedback risk. A deeper fit, custom earmold with a tuned vent, or programming changes can make even a semi-closed fit feel natural.</p>

<h3>How can I tell if the problem is occlusion or too much bass in my settings?</h3>
<p>Turn your hearing aids off and speak. If your voice still sounds boomy, it’s likely occlusion from the physical fit or venting. If the boominess mainly happens when the aids are on and improves when you lower volume, programming (low-frequency gain) may be the main issue.</p>

<h3>Do I need new hearing aids to fix occlusion?</h3>
<p>Usually not. Most occlusion problems are solved with different domes or vents, a deeper fit, custom sleeves or molds, and small programming adjustments. Real Ear Measurement during your visit helps target the exact fix without changing devices.</p>


